## Fresheyes Watchdog Protocol (MANDATORY for EVERY fresheyes invocation)

**The wall this prevents:** codex/fresheyes (Stage 2, and the spec/plan fresheyes loops) can hang indefinitely — process alive, near-zero CPU, no output — and a foreground `await` or a naive `until grep` waiter then blocks the whole pipeline for an unbounded time. This was observed stalling a run ~48 minutes before manual intervention. **A fresheyes call must never be able to stall the loop. Bound it in time, watch it for liveness, and reboot-or-fall-back automatically.**

Apply this to spec fresheyes, plan fresheyes, AND Stage-2 code fresheyes — every time.

**Rules:**
1. **Never block unbounded.** Do not run `fresheyes.sh` in the foreground with no timeout, and do not write an `until grep …; do sleep; done` waiter with no deadline. Both can wait forever. Always launch with stdin redirected from `/dev/null` — codex hangs forever reading a non-TTY stdin pipe.
2. **Hard ceiling: 20 minutes per fresheyes attempt.** Healthy high-reasoning reviews (gpt-5.6-sol, and gpt-5.5 before it) run 5–15 min. Past 20 min it is hung, not slow.
3. **Stall detection — watch the CODEX LOG, never fresheyes.sh's stdout.** `fresheyes.sh` buffers: its stdout stays **0 bytes for the entire healthy run** and only fills after codex exits. Watching stdout for growth false-positives on every run (this exact bug killed every review at ~90s on 2026-07-02). Liveness lives in the codex log under `$TMPDIR/fresheyes-logs/`. A fresheyes attempt is STALLED only when that log has not grown for **420 consecutive seconds** AND the verdict line is not yet present — gpt-5.5 at xhigh goes log-silent longer than the old gpt-5.3-codex (whose 2–4 min composing-silence set the earlier 300s), so do NOT lower this. The watchdog also checks the LOG for the verdict every tick and on exit: a review can COMPLETE (verdict written to the LOG) and then sit idle, tripping a naive flat-timer on an already-DONE run — always treat "verdict present in LOG" as DONE regardless of the timer.
4. **On stall or ceiling hit:** kill the whole fresheyes/codex process group, then **retry ONCE** from scratch. If the retry also stalls or hits the ceiling, **immediately fall back** to a fresh `general-purpose` subagent reviewer (no shared context) — do NOT retry a third time, do NOT wait, do NOT spiral. The fallback subagent is a first-class substitute for fresheyes (it satisfies the "independent cross-validation" requirement).
5. **Always emit a scorecard line** for the pass regardless of which reviewer produced it (fresheyes vs fallback subagent) — the loop must keep converging.
6. **Surface it briefly.** When you reboot or fall back, say so in one line ("fresheyes stalled at N min — rebooted / fell back to independent subagent"). Do not go silent.

**Reference implementation** (run fresheyes detached, watchdog it, kill+reboot on stall, else fall back):

```bash
# Launch fresheyes in its OWN PROCESS GROUP (set -m), writing to a known file.
# stdin MUST be </dev/null (codex hangs forever on a non-TTY pipe).
OUT=/tmp/fresheyes-$$.out
MARK=/tmp/fresheyes-$$.mark; touch "$MARK"   # timestamp marker: find THIS run's log, not a stale one
set -m
"<fresheyes.sh path>" "<scope>" >"$OUT" 2>&1 </dev/null &
FE_PID=$!   # with set -m this is also the process-group id
set +m
# $OUT stays 0 bytes until codex FINISHES (fresheyes.sh buffers) — liveness is in the codex log.
# Wait for a log NEWER THAN $MARK: `ls -t | head -1` after a fixed sleep races against
# stale/concurrent logs and false-stalls when codex takes >10s to create its log.
LOGDIR="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/fresheyes-logs"
LOG=""; waited=0
while [ -z "$LOG" ] && [ "$waited" -lt 180 ] && kill -0 "$FE_PID" 2>/dev/null; do
  sleep 5; waited=$((waited+5))
  LOG=$(find "$LOGDIR" -name '*.log' -newer "$MARK" 2>/dev/null | head -1)
done
if [ -z "$LOG" ]; then   # no log after 3 min (or process already dead without one) = broken launch
  kill -TERM -- "-$FE_PID" 2>/dev/null; echo "FRESHEYES_STALLED"; exit 0
fi
deadline=$(( $(date +%s) + 1200 ))   # 20-min hard ceiling; healthy reviews run 5-15 min
last_size=-1; flat=0; stalled=0
# The verdict lands in the codex LOG (fresheyes.sh only extracts to $OUT at the
# very end). Check the LOG *first* — a completed review may have finished then
# sat idle, tripping the flat-timer on an already-DONE run (observed 2026-07-05
# with gpt-5.5). $OUT is the fallback for the clean-exit path.
verdict_present() {
  grep -qE "INDEPENDENT CODE REVIEW (PASSED|FAILED)" "$LOG" 2>/dev/null \
    || grep -qE "INDEPENDENT CODE REVIEW (PASSED|FAILED)" "$OUT" 2>/dev/null
}
while kill -0 "$FE_PID" 2>/dev/null; do
  sleep 15
  now=$(date +%s); size=$(wc -c <"$LOG" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
  if [ "$size" = "$last_size" ]; then flat=$((flat+15)); else flat=0; fi
  last_size=$size
  if verdict_present; then break; fi   # already done — stop watching, don't kill
  # 420s tolerance: gpt-5.5 at xhigh goes log-silent LONGER than gpt-5.3-codex
  # (2-4 min was calibrated on the old model). Do NOT tighten below 420.
  if [ "$flat" -ge 420 ] || [ "$now" -ge "$deadline" ]; then stalled=1; break; fi
done
if verdict_present; then
  # Review completed (verdict in the LOG) regardless of flat-timer / buffering.
  kill -TERM -- "-$FE_PID" 2>/dev/null   # reap the idle process group; review is done
  echo "FRESHEYES_DONE"
elif [ "$stalled" = 1 ]; then
  # Kill the PROCESS GROUP only. NEVER `pkill -f "codex exec"` — that murders every
  # codex session on the machine, including ones the user is running elsewhere.
  kill -TERM -- "-$FE_PID" 2>/dev/null; sleep 2; kill -KILL -- "-$FE_PID" 2>/dev/null
  echo "FRESHEYES_STALLED"   # → retry once, then fall back to a general-purpose subagent
else
  wait "$FE_PID" 2>/dev/null
  verdict_present && echo "FRESHEYES_DONE" || echo "FRESHEYES_STALLED"
fi
# NOTE: when extracting the review, read the codex LOG if $OUT is empty — a
# flat-timer kill can leave $OUT unwritten while the full review sits in $LOG:
#   python3 - "$LOG" <<'PY'  (find last "## Files Examined" → end)
```

Run this watchdog block itself with `run_in_background: true` (Bash) so the pipeline is re-invoked on completion — but because it self-terminates at the 20-min ceiling, it can never hang the loop. When it prints `FRESHEYES_STALLED`, retry once; on a second stall, dispatch the `general-purpose` fallback reviewer instead. Never leave a hung `codex`/`fresheyes.sh` process alive — but teardown is **process-group-scoped only** (`kill -- -$FE_PID`). Do NOT use `pkill -f "codex exec"` or `pkill -f fresheyes.sh`: those match every codex/fresheyes process on the machine, including unrelated sessions the user has running.

